 {
  "year": "author note",
  "name": "What the two recent Amritapuri sets ask (read 25 Nov 2026 from the CodeChef statements; no editorials read)",
  "links": [],
  "note": "Sets of 11-12 problems. The half most teams solve is short-statement ad hoc work: invariants and parity on binary strings and stone piles, small constructions and casework (sums of composites, triplets of 1..3), simple two-player games decided by parity, counting with n up to 2000. The middle band is expectation over random orders, number theory with mod and gcd, grids with parity, and trees (pairings and path pairs counted per edge). The hard end is data-structure queries, interval problems and MST variants at n up to 10^6. Consequence for the course: games and per-edge tree counting were missing and were added; invariants, constructions and expectation need the most chains and problems."
 }
]
